From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bornaradusin <bornaradusin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 127/127] PC5: a game's installers and its built-in browser's processes
 start as processes of their own

A PS5 title can't create a process, so a program a game starts failed
to start (Wine patch 0550). PC5 now starts the ones that need no picture
of their own, hidden, through elfldr, which PC5 already uses for /data
(PC5's title/pc5_children.h):

- Wine patch 1000: with __wine_ps5_set_spawn set, NtCreateUserProcess
  goes on as on other systems for a game's installers (Visual C++,
  DirectX, .NET, PhysX, Windows Installer), a built-in web browser's
  processes (Chromium's, --type= in their command line) and anything a
  started program starts, and hands the new process's server socket to
  the title. The new process gets the environment without the launcher
  hand-off's, with winevulkan off and PW_CHILD=1.
- The server runs in the title and signals a thread by its kernel id: a
  started program's threads are in another process, so those signals go
  to the title (pw_wine_set_remote_kill), which sends them on to PC5's
  loader there, and the loader keeps that process's threads' table
  (__wine_ps5_set_thread_registry).
- wine64_main.c: in a test version of PC5, or with PW_PC5_CHILDREN=1, the
  title reads pc5child.elf and sets the hooks once ntdll is loaded, and
  every started program ends before the title restarts or closes.
